Completed a verified Track B generator based on the paper’s native projective-design objects.

- [Generator module](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2409.09730/gen_2409_09730.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2409.09730/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2409.09730/README.md)
- [Bare oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2409.09730/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Structural-hint transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2409.09730/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2409.09730/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Key results:

- All G1–G9 gates pass.
- Shipping preset: `medium`, \(d=72\), \(p=4099\).
- Four attacks each failed on 8/8 instances.
- Gaussian elimination solved 8/8 as expected: 42,650 field operations per instance.
- Compact route: 213 exact operations.
- Guessing: 0/200,000; exact solution density approximately \(3.16\times10^{-257}\).
- Oracle arms: bare 0/3, hinted 0/3, placebo 0/3.
- Answer size: 335 characters, 72 atoms.
- The initially held `easy` preset was correctly rejected after its structural hint enabled one oracle to solve it; the single permitted escalation to `medium` then hardened successfully.

The construction follows Lemma 4.1 and Remark 1 of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.09730v1), using hyperplane-stabilizer orbits under \(PGL(d,p)\).
